        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "That's a very nice suggestion! Thank you!\r\n\r\nI don't think having this built-in is undoubtedly a good thing (built-in mappings is already quite crowded). But it can be implemented via custom action. For example, something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal switch_picker = function()\r\n  local ok, picker_name = pcall(vim.fn.input, 'Picker name: ')\r\n  if not ok or MiniPick.registry[picker_name] == nil then return end\r\n\r\n  local query = MiniPick.get_picker_query()\r\n  MiniPick.stop()\r\n\r\n  vim.cmd('Pick ' .. picker_name)\r\n  local transfer_query = function() MiniPick.set_picker_query(query) end\r\n  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('User', { pattern = 'MiniPickStart', once = true, callback = transfer_query })\r\nend\r\n\r\nrequire('mini.pick').setup({\r\n  mappings = {\r\n    switch = { char = '<C-z>', func = switch_picker },\r\n  },\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith this you can press `<C-z>`, type a picker name you want to switch to (should be present in registry, i.e. executable via `:Pick` command), press `<CR>`, and it should switch picker while transferring the current query.\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nClosing as \"doable but seems to be too much to be built-in\". Although it is a very good candidate for a possible future wiki entry.",
